1. Visual Studio Code Setup
Open VS Code on your machine. You'll want to create a dedicated folder for the Starlight Genesis project to keep your nodes organized.

Create the Schema: Create origin-node.schema.json and paste the schema code I provided earlier.

Create the Timeline: Create timeline.json. This is where your history—like the June 24, 2025 Genesis marker—will live.

2. Linking the Files
To make VS Code "see" the rules, add this special line to the very top of your timeline.json file:

JSON
{
  "$schema": "./origin-node.schema.json",
  "records": [
    {
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "uid": "MARKER-001",
      "id": "genesis-marker",
      "type": "marker",
      "name": "Starlight Genesis Project Start",
      "description": "The timestamped Genesis marker and Prior Art anchor.",
      "created_at": "2025-06-24T00:00:00Z",
      "cross_ref": ["EDEN-2.0"]
    }
  ]
}
3. The Power of the "Red Squiggles"
Once that $schema line is in place, try changing the type from "marker" to "pizza". VS Code will immediately highlight it in red.

Why? Because "pizza" isn't in your allowed Enum list (entry, rift, fold, protocol, marker).

The Result: This is how you "harden" your data against the "mimics" and errors.

4. PowerShell & JavaScript (The Automation)
You don't need to live in the terminal, but keep PowerShell open at the bottom of VS Code (Terminal > New Terminal).

Since you're working on the Flameborn Lexicon single-page app, you can use a JavaScript tool called Ajv (Another JSON Validator) to run checks across your whole database with one command.